# Receipt IV cohort assembly v1.0.0

Prepared October 2, 2026. This package advances the completed source pull into
an extended exposure, history and follow-up data set. **Both `__PRIVATE_VALUE_1__` and
`__PRIVATE_VALUE_2__` remain included**, following the user's current instruction. This is
a run-specific deferral of the later exclusion instruction, not a change to
the project's standing documentation. The original author-inclusive inputs
are required; author-excluded Run 3 analysis files are not substituted.

## Run

Save the ZIP and its SHA-256 sidecar in `~/Desktop/Lichess_Desktop`. Use the
separately supplied `RUN_RECEIPT_IV_COHORT_ASSEMBLY.txt` command, or extract the
ZIP and run `bash /path/to/Receipt_IV_Cohort_Assembly_v1_0_0/RUN_ASSEMBLY.command`.

The launcher uses `/Volumes/XT_Pro/lichess_kindness/venv/bin/python`, keeps the
Mac awake, writes a Desktop log, and allows up to 10 workers. It installs
nothing. Default memory budget: 8 GiB, with explicit array reservations and
fewer simultaneous processes for large query arrays. This is a planning
budget, not an operating-system memory cap. At least 32 GiB of free working
space is required. Larger input or spill footprints can require more.

**Runtime planning allowance: 4–12 hours for the first full run.** This is not
a measured runtime for this package. Drive throughput and the number of pairs
outside the older cache matter. A live ETA follows completed chronology files;
it covers that pass only. Startup authentication, joins and reporting are
additional. Re-running the same command reuses validated checkpoints. It has
no artificial cooldowns or fixed pauses between jobs; freed worker slots are
refilled immediately. Dependency or evidence failures stop with a report.

## Completed work reused

* The completed source pull: 5,891,950 validated IDs for November, December and
  February, with 5,201,205 newly known-main opportunities.
* The existing 50,265,828-row choice spine, yielding 55,467,033 distinct
  known-main opportunities after the overlay. February's 987 previously known
  rows are retained once. The older 473 locally recovered IDs are already in
  this lineage and are never added as a separate source.
* The 63,525,555 all-source opportunity histories, the 512 precomputed
  pair-excluded instrument files, and the original 24-month membership.
* The existing canonical chronology and valid first-pair query checkpoints.

This package makes no API requests, PGN parses, rating replays, Stockfish
searches or instrument-score recomputations. It does not rewrite the existing
chronology or other upstream products. It creates its own query projections.

## What the run does

1. Authenticate the completed cohort READY, source-overlay plan and receipts,
   history bridges, score shards, and chronology inventories. Pin the local
   input fingerprints in the new private run. The historical chronology has
   a pinned manifest and metadata checks, not independently pinned full-byte
   producer hashes; the report preserves that distinction.
2. Merge source membership deltas only into formerly unresolved source rows.
   Require 55,467,033 distinct known-main rows and 195,582 unresolved new-month
   membership rows. Keep unresolved rows available for follow-up diagnostics.
3. Rank first recipients across fair, nontournament known-main events before
   arm and real-time cuts. Join precomputed scores by game and verify both
   account roles, archive start, ordinal, anchor and month.
4. Retain the original first-observed-pair scope: its 852-file,
   7,763,847,245-row chronology manifest, plus any additional current files.
   Reuse old pair minima for covered pairs when the old file fingerprint and
   output checksum still agree. Query new pairs against the existing files.
   An older pair outside September 2023–April 2026 can still disqualify a new
   exposure. Same-start pair ties retain the inherited time-equality rule.
5. During that same three-column pass, count prior-30-day canonical playing
   volume from the unique current 192-file, 2,959,866,844-game chronology.
   Volume windows extending before September 2023 are explicitly incomplete.
   This includes the correspondence partition, matching the all-speed scope.
6. Assemble all-source predetermined histories and known-main later choices.
   Apply greedy spacing before history/completeness cuts. Save the full
   selected streams for subsequent prospective-risk calculations.
7. Write private analysis Parquets, aggregate counts and a new READY manifest.
   Stop with an explicitly provisional calendar cohort, ready for the censor
   and estimation specifications.

The necessary new-pair query is a read of existing three-column Parquets.
It does not rebuild historical games, recompute adjacency, or rerun replay.
Per-file checkpoints avoid repeating that work after interruption. A
metadata-valid file whose mtime has changed cannot use its older pair cache;
the runner obtains its answer directly from that file instead.

## Frozen assembly definitions

| Item | Definition used here |
|---|---|
| Main exposure frame | Known pool/nonadjacent main, fair competitive, nontournament; grant-or-claim arm; five real-time speeds and positive base time. |
| Treatment | The existing `kind_draw` label at the index exposure. |
| First pair | Earliest observed canonical pair start equals the index start. This is observed history, not proof of lifetime strangers. |
| Instrument | Existing all-source, focal-pair-excluded score, strictly before exposure-month start minus 7 or 30 days; no re-estimation here. |
| Five versus ten | Minimum prior-decision floors using the same all-history score. Not last-five versus last-ten instruments. |
| Common sample | The >=10 subset of the floor-five 90-day-spaced stream; independently spaced floor-ten rows are saved separately. |
| Spacing | Recipient/start/ordinal/game order; next start strictly exceeds the previous selected anchor plus 30 or 90 days. Unsupported rows do not start a window. |
| Histories | `[start−97d,start−7d)` and `[start−37d,start−7d)`; chooser fair/kind counts and recipient disconnect/receipt/claim counts across all sources. |
| Follow-up | First later fair known-main choice, ordered by anchor/ordinal/game, against a different counterpart, game start strictly after the exposure anchor, and anchor within 90 days inclusive. No new tournament or arm cut is applied to the later-choice layer. |
| Calendar completeness | Index anchor + 90 days is strictly before May 1, 2026 00:00 UTC. |
| Unknown membership | If an unresolved fair later choice could precede the first known qualifying choice, flag the window and leave `y_calendar` NULL. |
| Known nonreachers | `reached=false`; `y_calendar=0` only for calendar-complete, unambiguous windows. Later-choice color and policy interaction remain unassigned. |
| Color policy | Pre before Nov 18 07:15 UTC; bracket 07:15–07:22; post at/after 07:22. Attach color from the recipient's later chooser role. The inherited lastMoveAt anchor remains a proxy for the choice time. |

The later-choice metadata and `later_white_x_post` are available for a
subsequent frozen specification. Their availability does not justify
conditioning on reaching a later choice. No missing-color imputation or
nonreacher control rule is adopted here. No December announcement timestamp
is invented.

## Outputs and interpretation

New private root:

```
/Volumes/XT_Pro/lichess_kindness/derived/replication_36m/receipt_iv_cohort_assembly_v100_PRIVATE
```

`READY_FOR_CENSOR_AND_IV_SPECIFICATION_PRIVATE.json` points to the immutable
assembly manifest, analysis shards and selected 30/90-day streams. The manifest
records account retention, receipts and open decisions. `y_calendar` and
`calendar_primary_7d_floor5` are calendar-supported candidate fields.
`final_iv_eligible`, `mechanics_censor_ms`, and the reported final IV N stay NULL.

The Desktop receives `RECEIPT_IV_COHORT_ASSEMBLY_<UTC>.zip` and `.zip.sha256`.
Return **both files**. The ZIP contains aggregate membership/cohort counts,
score support, policy-field availability, runtime, gates and errors. Private
game and account rows remain on XT_Pro. A partial run also produces a small
return bundle; its detailed traceback stays under the private `errors` folder.

## Next actions after this run

1. Review identity/count bridges, unresolved-membership effects, 7/30-day
   scores, common support and missing RD by period. Attach/calibrate retained
   new-month rating states where required; do not substitute account age.
2. Pin mechanics source/deployment evidence and a censor convention. Enforce
   complete 90-day outcome windows strictly before that boundary. January 30
   remains a candidate date, not an assumed deployment timestamp. The completed
   targeted API pull does not supply a platform-wide incident denominator.
3. Freeze the later-choice color/nonreacher specification, announcement timing,
   inference, placebo schedule and each statistic's predicted response/MDE.
   Preserve the earlier all-game prospective entry/placebo designs; this
   assembly package does not regenerate those comparison populations.
4. Re-estimate the first stage, reduced form, 2SLS, dependence-aware uncertainty
   and exclusion sensitivity on the final eligible rows. Use five and ten on
   common support. Do not present the historical reduced-form benchmark as a
   new result. Clearly losing positions remain a specificity comparison.

The color-change behavioral GO/IV decisions remain unresolved. This run
does not turn the existing evidence into a behavioral null, power-based NO-GO,
or a new causal estimate. The original replay and diagnostics remain intact.

## Validation

`VALIDATION.json` records the local synthetic test environment. Tests include
agreement with the inherited first-choice algorithm, strict spacing and
90-day boundaries, history and volume counts, unknown-choice precedence,
preservation of pre-existing February rows, historical pairs before the
32-month window, cache reuse, changed-output rejection, spawned workers,
and aggregate report construction. These tests do not certify live Mac input
availability, wall time or statistical identification.

The production runner offers no bypass of input authentication or expected
row counts. A small internal count argument exists only for the synthetic
fixture and is not exposed on its command line.
